                <p>
                    Release planning should continue after deployment. Reviewing release
                    performance helps teams understand what worked well, identify problems,
                    and improve future release planning and execution.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Review planned timelines against actual delivery performance.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Analyze production incidents, defects, and deployment issues.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Collect feedback from development, QA, operations, support, and business teams.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Evaluate customer feedback and product performance after release.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Document lessons learned and apply them to future release plans.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Continuously improve release processes, checklists, communication, and readiness criteria.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

